# Files excluded from the Function App deployment package.
#
# The runtime needs function_app.py, host.json, requirements.txt, the four
# first-party packages (services/, queries/, shared/, plus cost_database.py and
# database.py) and - load-bearing, easy to miss - the whole of sql/.
#
# sql/ is NOT build-time-only. The schema services read migrations off disk on
# each worker's first request (services/cost_schema_service.py:14,
# los_schema_service.py:15, supplement_schema_service.py:14,
# import_job_schema_service.py) and shared/sql_runner.py reads sql/export/ and
# sql/import/ for every dataset in shared/pipeline.py. It stays, all 161KB of it.
#
# Everything below is either the browser app, the test suite, operator scripts,
# or documentation. None of it is imported or read by a running function, and on
# Flex Consumption the package is mounted read-only at cold start, so shipping
# it buys nothing and lengthens the mount.

# Version control and CI
.git*
.github/
.vscode/

# Bytecode is deliberately NOT excluded here.
#
# A read-only package mount cannot have .pyc WRITTEN to it, which is exactly why
# shipping them matters: without them every cold start recompiles all ~417KB of
# first-party source, every time, and can never cache the result. Reading a .pyc
# from a read-only mount works fine. The CI build compiles them on purpose (see
# the compileall step in .github/workflows/main_los-functions.yml) with
# unchecked-hash invalidation, so a zip round trip cannot invalidate them on
# mtime.
#
# The one thing that makes them worthless is a magic-tag mismatch: a .pyc built
# by a different Python minor version than the runtime is silently ignored, not
# used. That is why PYTHON_VERSION in the workflow has to track the Function
# App's configured runtime version.
#
# .python_packages is a local `pip install --target` tree. Remote build resolves
# requirements.txt on Azure, so shipping a locally-built one only risks a
# platform mismatch.
.python_packages/

# Local development settings. Azure App Settings supply these in the cloud.
local.settings.json

# The browser application. Static Web Apps deploys frontend/ from its own
# workflow (.github/workflows/azure-static-web-apps-*.yml, app_location: /frontend).
frontend/
node_modules/
package.json
package-lock.json

# Test suite and its fixtures
tests/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage

# Operator and one-off scripts. Run from a checkout, not from the deployed app.
scripts/
backfill_supplement.py
benchmark_los.py
benchmark_supplement_grid.py
migrate_supplement.py
profile_supplement_source.py
validate_los_read_model.py
validate_supplement_parity.py

# Captured profiler output, kept for reference in the repo only.
supplement-profile.json

# Documentation
*.md
docs/

# Empty legacy v1 function directories, superseded by the v2 model in
# function_app.py. They hold nothing but stale bytecode.
costdata/
TimerFunc/
